<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lower Brittany</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Basse-Bretagne</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lower Brittany (Basse-Bretagne) is the western half of Brittany, where the Breton language was traditionally spoken, in contrast to Upper Brittany in the east, where Gallo and French prevailed. It covers roughly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finistere">Finistère</a> and the western parts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotes-darmor">Côtes-d'Armor</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morbihan">Morbihan</a>. The language border between the two moved slowly westward from the ninth century onwards.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lower-brittany.jpg" alt="Lower Brittany" width="320" height="233" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Breton_dialectes.svg: Pmx · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Breton_dialectes-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
